 "panel_id": "site_loss",
 "description": "v66 re-issue on the spatial candidate design, same panel id. Corner case: a spot fire ignites the region of the first (targets 1) or of both (targets 2) initial shelters at minute 5 or 35. The ignited region is the new hazard's source region, so its danger follows elapsed burn time and reaches the failure level 4 thirty minutes later: the targeted shelters are overrun at minute 34 or 64 in every tape and under every policy, and their occupants and reservation holders must re-evacuate. Adding the ignition draws no random number: wind, intensity, closures, other ignitions and evacuee origins equal the base episode of the same tape (the main panel's tapes: scenario stream 500, policy stream 600, pairs 1-20); only the primary fires' spread after the spot fire starts spreading is re-drawn from the shared hazard stream. Policy comparisons within a cell are exact common-random-number comparisons. Targets 1 removes 300 of 3,000 places (the N-1 design still covers 2,500 people); targets 2 removes 600, so at least 100 people cannot be sheltered. Outcome: the paired site-loss penalty J(stressed) - J(base) per policy, and the policy contrasts of that penalty (adaptive vs precommitted; front-aware vs overrun-blind; RL vs front-aware greedy). Analysis: scripts/site_loss_results.py.",
